.TH PMOD_STREAM 7 "7 November 2026" "v1.0" "PModPirate"
.SH NAME
pmod_stream \- Pmod pin-stream player
.SH SUMMARY
Catalog IP
.B user.org:user:pmod_stream:1.0 .
AXI4-Lite slave, 64 KB window, 8-bit bidirectional Pmod port.
The player consumes a run-length stream from block RAM and drives the pins on
.B play_clk .
Software fills the stream.
It does not bit-bang the pins.
.PP
.TS
box expand tab(;);
lb lb
l lw(42).
Item;Value
Play timebase;play_clk, 200 MHz in the Cmod S7 reference system
AXI clock;s_axi_aclk, independent of play_clk
Port width;8 bits, per-pin output enable
TX memory;8192 words (32 KB) at the default parameter
Capture;8192 samples, one byte per played word
Word format;{OE[7:0], DOUT[7:0], duration[15:0]}
Duration;1 to 65535 play_clk cycles (5 ns to 327.675 us at 200 MHz)
Sample point;last cycle of each word, after a 2-flop synchronizer
Idle;IDLE_OE and IDLE_OUT while not playing. Reset forces Hi-Z
Interrupt;level high, done or fault, while CTRL.irq_en is set
.TE
.SH DESCRIPTION
A Pmod header is an 8-bit digital connector.
Protocols such as SPI, I2C, UART, and timed GPIO are produced by writing a list of pin states and how long each state lasts.
.B pmod_stream
walks that list on
.B play_clk
and records the pin levels at the end of every entry.
.PP
While the player is idle, the pins follow
.B IDLE_OE
and
.B IDLE_OUT .
A 1 in
.B IDLE_OE
drives the matching
.B IDLE_OUT
bit.
A 0 releases the pin.
Both registers reset to zero, so the port comes out of reset Hi-Z.
The end of a play, including a fault and an abort, restores those idle levels.
.PP
Open-drain protocols are a property of the stream, not a pin mode in the IP.
A zero is written as output-enable 1 and data 0.
A one is written as output-enable 0.
The player never has to drive a high on that pin.
An external or on-board pull-up supplies the high level.
.SH PORTS
.TS
box expand tab(;);
lb lb lb lw(28).
Port;Direction;Clock;Description
s_axi_*;slave;s_axi_aclk;AXI4-Lite, 16-bit address, 32-bit data
s_axi_aclk;in;-;Register and memory port A clock
s_axi_aresetn;in;s_axi_aclk;Active-low AXI reset
play_clk;in;-;Player timebase
play_aresetn;in;async, synced to play_clk;Active-low player reset
pmod_io[7:0];inout;play_clk;Pmod data pins
irq;out;s_axi_aclk;Level-high interrupt
status_led;out;s_axi_aclk;Busy, or a heartbeat while idle
.TE
.PP
.B s_axi
is associated with
.B s_axi_aclk
and
.B s_axi_aresetn .
.B play_clk
is associated with
.B play_aresetn .
The two clocks are asynchronous.
Start, abort, done, idle levels, busy, and the pin sample cross between them.
.B irq
is level high.
It stays asserted while
.B CTRL.irq_en
is set and either
.B STATUS.done
or
.B STATUS.fault
is set.
.SH PARAMETERS
.TS
box expand tab(;);
lb lb lb lb.
Name;Default;Legal;Meaning
TX_WORDS;8192;up to 8192;Number of stream words in the TX RAM
RX_SAMPLES;8192;multiple of 4, up to 8192;Number of captured pin bytes
.TE
.PP
The address map below is fixed for the default sizes.
.B TX_WORDS
above 8192 does not fit the 13-bit word index.
.B RX_SAMPLES
is packed four bytes per AXI word, so it must be a multiple of four.
The packaged default is the size used by the PModPirate reference system.
.SH "STREAM WORD"
Each TX location is one 32-bit little-endian word.
A write must have all four byte strobes set.
A partial strobe is ignored.
.PP
.TS
box expand tab(;);
lb lb lb.
Bits;Field;Meaning
31:24;OE;1 = drive pmod_io[i], 0 = Hi-Z
23:16;DOUT;Level driven where OE is 1
15:0;DURATION;play_clk cycles this word is held, minimum 1
.TE
.PP
The word is applied on a rising edge of
.B play_clk
and remains on the pins for
.B DURATION
cycles.
On the edge where that count expires, the player stores the synchronized pin sample and either applies the next word or returns to idle.
The last word is not held for an extra cycle.
.PP
A duration of 0 is a fault.
The play stops and the pins return to idle.
Holds longer than 65535 cycles are several words with the same OE and DOUT.
At 200 MHz the longest single word is 327.675 us.
.SH CAPTURE
On the last cycle of each word the player writes one byte into the capture RAM.
Bit
.I i
of that byte is
.B pmod_io[i]
after two
.B play_clk
flops.
The byte for word
.I n
is stored at sample index
.I n .
.B CAP_COUNT
reports how many samples the play stored.
.PP
Because the sample passes through two flops, a level that changes on the same edge that starts a very short word can still show the previous level.
A word whose duration is at least 4 play clocks shows a level that became valid at the start of that word.
Place a word boundary on the instant that matters, and give that word enough cycles for the synchronizer.
Mid-bit of a UART character, the clock-high side of SPI, and the SCL-high window of an I2C ACK are the usual places.
Samples from other words are ignored by the consumer.
.PP
Four samples are packed into one AXI word, little-endian.
Sample
.I i
is byte
.I "(i mod 4)"
of the word at the RX base plus
.I "4 * (i / 4)" .
Byte 0 is bits 7:0.
.SH "REGISTER MAP"
Offsets are bytes from the base of the slave.
The slave window is 64 KB.
In the Cmod S7 reference system that base is
.B 0x00010000 ,
directly above the 64 KB MicroBlaze local memory.
.PP
.TS
box expand tab(;);
lb lb lb lw(34).
Offset;Name;Access;Description
0x0000;CTRL;R/W;bit 0 start, bit 1 abort, bit 2 irq_en
0x0004;STATUS;R/W1C;bit 0 busy, bit 1 done, bit 2 fault
0x0008;LENGTH;R/W;Number of TX words to play, bits 15:0
0x000C;CAP_COUNT;R;Samples stored by the last play, bits 15:0
0x0010;IDLE_OUT;R/W;Idle drive data, bits 7:0
0x0014;IDLE_OE;R/W;Idle output enable, bits 7:0. 1 = drive
0x0018;PIN_IN;R;Synchronized pin sample, bits 7:0
0x1000;TX;R/W;8192 words, through 0x8FFC
0x9000;RX;R;2048 packed words, through 0xAFFC
.TE
.PP
Reserved bits read as 0.
Writes to reserved bits are ignored.
AXI responses are OKAY.
.B CTRL
reads back
.B irq_en
in bit 2.
Start and abort are pulses, so those bits read as 0.
.PP
.B STATUS.done
and
.B STATUS.fault
are sticky.
Write 1 to the bit to clear it.
Writing 0 leaves it.
.B STATUS.busy
is read-only.
.PP
.B PIN_IN
is the capture synchronizer crossed back to
.B s_axi_aclk .
It is the register for a GPIO read that does not need a timed sample.
It is not a substitute for the capture RAM during a play.
.PP
TX reads and writes use the full 32-bit word.
RX is read-only.
A TX write with
.B WSTRB
other than
.B 0xF
does not change memory.
.SH "PROGRAMMING A PLAY"
.IP 1. 3
Write the words at
.B TX ,
starting at offset 0x1000.
.IP 2.
Write
.B LENGTH
with the word count.
Do this before start.
The value is copied across the clock domain with the start pulse.
.IP 3.
Clear the previous result by writing
.B 0x6
to
.B STATUS .
.IP 4.
Write
.B CTRL
bit 0.
Bit 2 may be set in the same write to enable the interrupt.
.IP 5.
Poll
.B STATUS
until
.B done
is set and
.B busy
is clear, or wait for
.B irq .
.IP 6.
If
.B fault
is clear, read
.B CAP_COUNT
and the packed samples at
.B RX .
.PP
A second start while
.B busy
is set, or while a start is still crossing into
.B play_clk ,
sets
.B fault
and does not start another play.
Wait for
.B done
before starting again.
.PP
Abort by writing
.B CTRL
bit 1.
The player returns to idle, sets
.B done ,
and does not set
.B fault
for the abort itself.
.SH FAULTS
.B STATUS.fault
is set, the pins return to idle, and
.B done
is set, when any of the following happens.
.IP \(bu 2
.B LENGTH
is 0 or greater than
.B TX_WORDS .
No word is driven.
.IP \(bu
A played word has duration 0.
Words before it were driven.
.IP \(bu
The next word is not available on the cycle a word expires.
That is an underrun.
With the default memory and a prefetch queue of four words it is not expected for a legal duration of at least 1.
.IP \(bu
.B CTRL.start
is written while a play is already active or a start is still pending.
.PP
.B CAP_COUNT
then reports how many samples were stored before the stop.
An abort is not a fault.
.SH "CLOCK, RESET, AND STATUS LED"
.B play_aresetn
is synchronized into
.B play_clk
before it resets the player.
.B s_axi_aresetn
resets the AXI registers directly.
Either reset clears output enable, so the pins are Hi-Z, and clears
.B irq_en ,
.B done ,
and
.B fault .
.PP
Idle levels use a multi-flop array synchronizer.
A write to
.B IDLE_OE
or
.B IDLE_OUT
is visible on the pins a few
.B play_clk
cycles later, and only while the player is idle.
During a play the stream overrides them.
.PP
.B status_led
is high while
.B STATUS.busy
is set.
While idle it follows bit 25 of a counter on
.B s_axi_aclk .
At 100 MHz that bit is high for about 0.34 s and low for about 0.34 s.
.SH "REFERENCE SYSTEM"
On the Cmod S7-25 design the slave is at
.B 0x00010000
and the UART is a separate peripheral.
.B s_axi_aclk
is 100 MHz.
.B play_clk
is 200 MHz.
Both come from an MMCM whose input is the 12 MHz board clock.
The interrupt is connected to the MicroBlaze interrupt controller.
The firmware polls
.B STATUS
and leaves
.B irq_en
clear.
The eight Pmod pins are LVCMOS33 with pull-ups.
The status LED is a board LED.
.PP
Those choices belong to the board design.
The IP itself only requires a stable
.B play_clk ,
an AXI clock, and the two active-low resets.
.SH UTILIZATION
Out-of-context synthesis of the default configuration, Vivado 2025.2, part xc7s25csga225-1:
.PP
.TS
box expand tab(;);
lb lb lb.
Resource;Used;Device
Slice LUTs;361;14600
Slice registers;542;29200
RAMB36;16;45
RAMB18;4;90
DSP;0;80
.TE
.PP
The block RAM holds the 32 KB TX stream and the 8 KB capture buffer.
The count is what inference produced for the true dual-port TX RAM and the four byte-wide capture banks.
It is a synthesis result, not a placed-and-routed total for the whole design.
.SH REVISION
.TS
box expand tab(;);
lb lb lw(40).
Version;Date;Notes
1.0;2026-11-07;First packaged player. Default 8192 words and 8192 samples.
.TE
